From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: marty <marty@fyai.co.nz>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 20:46:11 +1200
Subject: [PATCH] RetroPlayer: size the hardware framebuffer from the stream,
 not from the client

The client never asks for a size - CVideoStream::GetHwFramebuffer() in the
wrapper calls GetBuffer(0, 0), because a hardware client renders into whatever
it is handed. That reached CRenderBufferFBO::Allocate() as 0x0, which it
refuses, so no buffer was created, GetCurrentFramebuffer() returned 0, and the
client drew to framebuffer 0 - which on our surfaceless context is nowhere at
all. Black screen, no errors, Kodi rendering happily at 60fps.

Falls back to the dimensions the stream was configured with.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 xbmc/cores/RetroPlayer/rendering/RPRenderManager.cpp | 11 +++++++++++
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+)

diff --git a/xbmc/cores/RetroPlayer/rendering/RPRenderManager.cpp b/xbmc/cores/RetroPlayer/rendering/RPRenderManager.cpp
index a492ffd..09d1cb8 100644
--- a/xbmc/cores/RetroPlayer/rendering/RPRenderManager.cpp
+++ b/xbmc/cores/RetroPlayer/rendering/RPRenderManager.cpp
@@ -349,6 +349,17 @@ uintptr_t CRPRenderManager::GetCurrentFramebuffer(unsigned int width, unsigned i
   // waiting for one deadlocks at a black screen with the client emulating
   // happily into nothing. The software path gets away with the gate because
   // AddFrame() caches frames until the GUI catches up.
+  // The wrapper asks with 0x0: CVideoStream::GetHwFramebuffer() calls
+  // GetBuffer(0, 0) because a hardware client never states a size - it renders
+  // into whatever it is given. Fall back to what the stream was configured
+  // with, or the pool refuses to allocate and the client silently draws to
+  // framebuffer 0, which on our surfaceless context is nowhere.
+  if (width == 0 || height == 0)
+  {
+    width = m_nominalWidth;
+    height = m_nominalHeight;
+  }
+
   for (IRenderBufferPool* bufferPool : m_hwBufferPools)
   {
     IRenderBuffer* renderBuffer = bufferPool->GetBuffer(width, height);
